    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The town of Panyu was founded in 214 BCE as the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> commandery, became the capital of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo">Zhao Tuo</a> in 204 BCE and was annexed by the Han in 111 BCE.</li><li>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> it was a port tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and from 1757 to 1842 the Thirteen Factories there were the only legal place to conduct foreign trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</li><li>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> it lost its monopoly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, but it remained an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> and became a base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> in the 1920s.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The city's nicknames, the City of Rams and the City of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immortals">Immortals</a>, come from a story of five immortals who rode five rams and introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation to the area, around the time of the city's foundation.</p></aside>
    </section>

