    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">214 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Panyu is founded on the eastern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a> as the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin Empire</a>'s Nanhai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">204 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo">Zhao Tuo</a> establishes the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a> and makes Panyu its capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">111 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Han armies take Panyu and annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">758</span><span class="rf-text">Arab and Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> sack the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">878</span><span class="rf-text">The rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> takes the city and about 200,000 foreigners are killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1757</span><span class="rf-text">The Thirteen Factories become the only legal place for foreign trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1841</span><span class="rf-text">The British capture the city during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text">Communist forces take Guangzhou on 14 October</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Guangzhou stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a>, on its eastern bank in the old town near Baiyun Mountain, about 80 miles (130 kilometres) from the river's junction with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>. The old town commanded the alluvial plain of the Pearl River Delta, with its connection to the sea protected at the Humen Strait. Before the Han conquest the area was inhabited by indigenous, non-Chinese tribes known collectively as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a>. A settlement now known as Nanwucheng was present in the area by 1100 BCE, and some traditional Chinese histories placed its founding in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-nan-of-zhou">King Nan of Zhou</a> (314 to 256 BCE). It was said to have been little more than a stockade of bamboo and mud.</p><p>The town known as Panyu was founded on the eastern bank of the Pearl River in 214 BCE. The origin of the name Panyu is uncertain; eleven explanations have been offered, including that it referred to two local mountains. The city's nicknames come from the Temple of the Five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immortals">Immortals</a>, where five stones were said to be the rams ridden by the Taoist culture heroes credited with introducing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation around the time of the city's foundation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone.jpg" alt="Odoric of Pordenone" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone">Odoric of Pordenone</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DepartureOfOdoricOfPordenone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Panyu was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin Empire</a>'s Nanhai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a> and served as a base for the first invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> lands in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a> it was a commercial centre: archaeologists have found in it remains from central China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The Old Book of Tang described Guangzhou as an important port in southern China, and direct routes connected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and China. Medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-masudi">Al-Masudi</a> and Ibn Khordadbeh knew the city as Khanfu.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone">Odoric of Pordenone</a> wrote that Guangzhou was as large as three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venices</a> in area and rivalled all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the amount of crafts it produced. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in the 14th century and described how the Chinese built their large ships in the port's shipyards. After the Qing took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1683 foreign trade revived: the Portuguese, Spaniards, English, French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, Danish and Swedish companies and later American ships came. By then Guangzhou was one of the world's greatest ports, organised under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canton-system">Canton System</a>, and its main exports were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>. The Thirteen Factories were the only legal place to conduct foreign trade with China from 1757 to 1842.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Guangzhou&#8221; cites where it covers Imperial China, Ethnicity and language:</p><ul><li>Frederic Jr. Wakeman, The Great Enterprise: The Manchu Reconstruction of Imperial Order in Seventeenth-Century China (University of California Press, 1985)</li><li>Geoffrey Gunn, History without Borders: The Making of an Asian World Region, 1000–1800 (Hong Kong University Press, 2011)</li><li>Richard Von Glahn, Fountain of Fortune: Money and Monetary Policy in China, 1000–1700 (University of California Press, 1996)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhao-mo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhao-mo.jpg" alt="Zhao Mo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zhao-mo">Zhao Mo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Si_l%C3%BC_yu_yi.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Panyu was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin Empire</a>'s Nanhai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a>. When the Qin fell, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo">Zhao Tuo</a> established the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a> and made Panyu its capital in 204 BCE. In 196 BCE he negotiated recognition of his independence in exchange for nominal submission to the Han. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-mo">Zhao Mo</a> and Zhao Yingqi, the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-jia">Lü Jia</a> killed the Han ambassadors and the king Zhao Xing in a coup, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> sent six armies that took Panyu and annexed Nanyue by the end of 111 BCE. Panyu then became a provincial capital, and in 226 CE it became the seat of Guang Prefecture, which gave the city its modern name.</p><p>After the Tang, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a> governor Liu Yan used Panyu as the base of a Great Yue or Southern Han empire that lasted from 917 to 971, and the city was renamed Xingwang Fu. In 1646 Zhu Yuyue declared himself the Shaowu Emperor there as the last stronghold of the Ming empire. Under the Republic it was the seat of governments led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>, and from 1923 to 1926 the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>. It briefly served as capital of the Republican government in 1949.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The area's population was at first the indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a>. The 19th-century city was mostly of one- or two-storey buildings, with a few exceptions such as the Flower Pagoda of the Temple of the Six Banyan Trees and the Five-Story Pagoda. Brick walls about 6 miles (10 kilometres) in circumference, 25 feet high and 20 feet wide enclosed it, with eight main gates and two water gates guarded by day and closed at night. A partition wall divided the northern old town from the southern new town, and the suburb of Xiguan lay beyond. Boats of fishers, traders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanka">Tanka</a> boat people almost entirely concealed about 4 miles of the riverbank. Homes often had a storefront facing the street and used their courtyards as warehouses.</p><p>The city held many faiths. The Zhizhi Temple was founded in 233 and has been known as the Guangxiao Temple since the Ming. The Huaisheng Mosque is said to have been built in 627 by Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, and the city had communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a> in the Tang. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christians</a> arrived along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> but were essentially extinct by the year 1000. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-heart">Sacred Heart</a> Cathedral was built from 1861 to 1888. Cantonese is the local language, with Cantonese cuisine, opera and embroidery, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> population also lived in the surrounding districts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Arab and Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> sacked the city on 30 October 758, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan-rebellion">An Lushan Rebellion</a>. In the Guangzhou massacre of 878 the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> killed about 200,000 Arab, Persian and other foreigners, along with the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a>, and the port was closed for fifty years. The Ming imposed sea bans, and the maritime intendancy of Guangzhou was closed in 1384. When the Portuguese arrived in 1516 and the following year, and were accused of piracy and the enslavement of Chinese children, the Guangzhou administration defeated them at the Battle of Tunmen and executed 23 by lingchi.</p><p>On 20 January 1647 the Ming turncoat Li Chengdong sacked the city for the Qing and executed Zhu Yuyue. After a prolonged siege, Shang Kexi's forces captured it on 24 November 1650 and killed up to 70,000 Ming loyalists and civilians. The British captured it on 18 March 1841 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a>. The Punti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakka-people">Hakka</a> waged clan wars from 1855 to 1867 in which one million people died, and the foreign trade facilities were destroyed in the Arrow War of 1856 to 1858. The Japanese occupied it by the end of December 1938. In 1949 retreating Nationalist forces blew up the Haizhu Bridge, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> many temples, churches and other monuments were destroyed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen.jpg" alt="Sun Yat-sen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%AD%99%E4%B8%AD%E5%B1%B1%E8%82%96%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">上海波尔照相馆, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Guangzhou's trade shaped the modern world economy: as a meeting place of merchants from all over the world it became a major contributor to the rise of the modern global economy. After the 1842 treaty it lost its privileged trade status, and trade moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, though it remained an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a>. In June 1919 the demolition of the city wall began to make way for wider streets and tramways, and it took three years. Its revolutionary history includes the uprisings of 1895 and 1911, honoured in the 72 Martyrs of Huanghuagang Park, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whampoa-military-academy">Whampoa Military Academy</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> trained an armed force with Soviet help.</p><p>Cantonese, Cantonese cuisine and Cantonese opera belong to the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a> region. Many overseas Chinese have ties to the city, particularly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. After 1949 housing was built on the banks of the Pearl River to settle the boat people on land. In 2000 Huadu and Panyu joined the municipality, and the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 18,676,605 people in its administrative area. Guangzhou is today the capital of Guangdong and the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a> Delta.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/glory-to-hong-kong">Glory to Hong Kong<span>2019</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Guangzhou, founded as Panyu in 214 BCE, was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a>, a port tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and from 1757 to 1842 the only legal site of foreign trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It later became a revolutionary base and is now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>.</em></p>

